-- Idea cards, batch 1 (Nov 1 2026): 36 cards for the first testers.
-- Every card is a summary in our own words, credited to its source. The only direct quote is from
-- Walden (1854, public domain). Each ends with a question to start a conversation.
-- Safe to re-run (cards are matched on source + title and updated in place).

select public.put_idea_cards($json$[
{"title":"Systems beat goals","source_title":"Atomic Habits","source_author":"James Clear","topics":["fitness","entrepreneurship"],
 "body":"Everyone in a race wants to win, so the goal isn't what separates people. What you do every day is. Make good habits obvious and easy, make bad ones hard to reach, and let tiny gains add up. What's one small thing you do daily that's quietly paying off?"},
{"title":"Every action is a vote for who you're becoming","source_title":"Atomic Habits","source_author":"James Clear","topics":["fitness","meaning"],
 "body":"Instead of chasing an outcome (run a marathon), decide who you want to be (a runner) and prove it with small wins. Each workout, page or call is a vote. You don't need a unanimous vote, just a majority. What identity are you voting for right now?"},
{"title":"Expect difficult people, and don't let them change you","source_title":"Meditations","source_author":"Marcus Aurelius","topics":["stoicism","philosophy","leadership"],
 "body":"An emperor reminded himself each morning that he'd run into rude, ungrateful and selfish people that day, and that none of them could make him act badly. We're built to work together, like two hands. Who tested your patience lately, and how did you handle it?"},
{"title":"The obstacle becomes the path","source_title":"The Obstacle Is the Way","source_author":"Ryan Holiday","topics":["stoicism","entrepreneurship"],
 "body":"What stands in the way can be turned into the way forward. See the problem clearly without panic, act on the part you control, and accept what you can't. Many businesses were born from a setback someone refused to waste. What setback ended up pushing you forward?"},
{"title":"Build the smallest thing that tests your idea","source_title":"The Lean Startup","source_author":"Eric Ries","topics":["startups","entrepreneurship","product-design"],
 "body":"Don't spend a year building something nobody asked for. Find the riskiest guess behind your idea, build the smallest version that tests it, measure what people actually do, and learn fast. Build, measure, learn, repeat. What's the riskiest assumption in what you're working on?"},
{"title":"Compliments aren't customers","source_title":"The Mom Test","source_author":"Rob Fitzpatrick","topics":["startups","sales","small-business"],
 "body":"Ask people if your idea is good and they'll be nice to you. Instead, ask about their life: the last time they had the problem, what they tried, what they paid. Past behavior is data; opinions about the future aren't. Who could you have that kind of conversation with this week?"},
{"title":"What do you believe that few people agree with?","source_title":"Zero to One","source_author":"Peter Thiel","topics":["startups","venture-capital","entrepreneurship"],
 "body":"Copying what works takes the world from 1 to n. Creating something new takes it from 0 to 1, and that usually starts with a belief most people don't share yet. The best businesses are built on secrets hiding in plain sight. What's your unpopular but true opinion?"},
{"title":"Wealth is what you don't see","source_title":"The Psychology of Money","source_author":"Morgan Housel","topics":["personal-finance","investing"],
 "body":"The flashy car shows money spent, not money kept. Real wealth is the savings, options and freedom you never show off. Getting rich and staying rich are different skills, and leaving room for error matters more than squeezing every return. What does 'enough' look like to you?"},
{"title":"A 'why' can carry almost any 'how'","source_title":"Man's Search for Meaning","source_author":"Viktor Frankl","topics":["meaning","existentialism"],
 "body":"A psychiatrist who survived the camps saw that people with something to live for, someone waiting or work left to finish, held on longer. He argued meaning comes from what we create, who we love, and the attitude we choose in hard times. What's your 'why' right now?"},
{"title":"Focus is a superpower now","source_title":"Deep Work","source_author":"Cal Newport","topics":["careers","software"],
 "body":"The ability to concentrate on hard things without distraction is getting rarer just as it's becoming more valuable. Block real time for your most important work, protect it, and treat email and busywork as what they are. When do you do your best focused work?"},
{"title":"If it isn't a clear yes, it's a no","source_title":"Essentialism","source_author":"Greg McKeown","topics":["leadership","careers"],
 "body":"Saying yes to everything spreads you a millimetre deep in a hundred directions. Do less, but better: pick the few things that matter most and say no to the rest, kindly and without guilt. What's something you should say no to this month?"},
{"title":"Work on your business, not just in it","source_title":"The E-Myth Revisited","source_author":"Michael E. Gerber","topics":["small-business","trades","entrepreneurship"],
 "body":"Most small businesses are started by skilled tradespeople who end up with a job, not a business, because everything depends on them. Write down how things get done, build simple systems, and make it run without you. What would your business need to run for a week without you?"},
{"title":"Make an offer people feel stupid saying no to","source_title":"$100M Offers","source_author":"Alex Hormozi","topics":["sales","marketing","small-business"],
 "body":"People buy when the result they want is big, they believe they'll get it, it comes fast, and it takes little effort on their part. Raise the first two, shrink the last two, and price stops being the main question. How could you make your offer faster or easier for the buyer?"},
{"title":"Name the feeling, then ask 'how'","source_title":"Never Split the Difference","source_author":"Chris Voss","topics":["sales","leadership"],
 "body":"A former FBI hostage negotiator's tools: say out loud what the other person seems to feel ('It seems like you're worried about…'), and ask open 'how' and 'what' questions so they help solve your problem. Empathy is a tactic, not a weakness. Got a tough negotiation coming up?"},
{"title":"Share the process, not just the result","source_title":"Show Your Work!","source_author":"Austin Kleon","topics":["art","marketing","product-design"],
 "body":"You don't need to be an expert to share what you're learning. Show the messy middle, what you're trying and what broke, and people who care about the same things will find you. Being an amateur in public is how many careers start. What are you building that you haven't shown anyone?"},
{"title":"Generalists win in a messy world","source_title":"Range","source_author":"David Epstein","topics":["careers","reading"],
 "body":"Early specialists shine in tidy fields with clear rules. In messy, changing ones, people who sampled widely and connected ideas from different places often do better. A winding path isn't wasted time. What's an odd skill from one part of your life that helps in another?"},
{"title":"Your fast brain tells convincing stories","source_title":"Thinking, Fast and Slow","source_author":"Daniel Kahneman","topics":["logic","mind","investing"],
 "body":"We have a quick, intuitive mode and a slow, careful one. The quick one builds a confident story from whatever it happens to see and ignores what it doesn't. Big decisions deserve the slow mode: what am I missing? What's a decision you made too fast?"},
{"title":"Some things get stronger from stress","source_title":"Antifragile","source_author":"Nassim Nicholas Taleb","topics":["investing","philosophy","fitness"],
 "body":"Fragile things break under shocks, sturdy things resist them, and some things, like muscles, gain from them. Take small, survivable risks, avoid the ones that can wipe you out, and keep options open. What part of your life is set up to benefit from chaos?"},
{"title":"We stopped joining things, and it costs us","source_title":"Bowling Alone","source_author":"Robert D. Putnam","topics":["community","volunteering"],
 "body":"Since the 1960s, membership in clubs, leagues and local groups fell sharply in America. Those groups built trust and connection between neighbours, and losing them made people lonelier and less healthy. What group would you actually show up for every week?"},
{"title":"Give every gathering a real purpose","source_title":"The Art of Gathering","source_author":"Priya Parker","topics":["community","networking"],
 "body":"Most get-togethers are forgettable because their purpose is generic ('let's catch up'). Decide what this gathering is for and who it's for, and host with confidence instead of staying hands-off. A specific purpose makes better nights. What's the best gathering you've ever been to?"},
{"title":"Be interested, not interesting","source_title":"How to Win Friends and Influence People","source_author":"Dale Carnegie","topics":["networking","leadership"],
 "body":"You'll make more friends by being genuinely curious about other people than by trying to impress them. Remember names, ask about what they care about, and let them talk. It's simple and most people still don't do it. Who's someone you'd like to know better?"},
{"title":"The real price of things","source_title":"Walden","source_author":"Henry David Thoreau","topics":["meaning","camping","personal-finance"],
 "quote":"I went to the woods because I wished to live deliberately.",
 "body":"Thoreau spent two years in a small cabin to see what life really needs. His test for anything you buy: how much of your life do you trade for it, now or later? Simplify, and you buy back time. What would you cut to buy back more of your life?"},
{"title":"Started from the trunk of a car","source_title":"Shoe Dog","source_author":"Phil Knight","topics":["startups","entrepreneurship"],
 "body":"Nike began with a young runner importing Japanese shoes and selling them out of his car at track meets. For years it nearly went broke again and again. The founder's memoir is mostly about not stopping. What's the 'trunk of a car' version of your idea?"},
{"title":"There's no recipe for the hard parts","source_title":"The Hard Thing About Hard Things","source_author":"Ben Horowitz","topics":["leadership","startups"],
 "body":"Books tell you how to set goals; nobody tells you what to do when everything is going wrong. The struggle is normal, and the people who get through it face bad news fast and tell their team the truth. What's the hardest call you've had to make?"},
{"title":"Play long-term games with long-term people","source_title":"The Almanack of Naval Ravikant","source_author":"Eric Jorgenson","topics":["entrepreneurship","investing","side-hustles"],
 "body":"Build skills that are hard to teach, use leverage like code and content that work while you sleep, and stick with people you trust for the long haul. Returns compound in relationships just like in money. Who's someone you'd want to build with for the next ten years?"},
{"title":"You're probably only at 40%","source_title":"Can't Hurt Me","source_author":"David Goggins","topics":["fitness","martial-arts"],
 "body":"When your mind says you're done, you've likely used only a fraction of what you have. Look at yourself honestly, set hard targets, and do the uncomfortable thing on purpose so you're ready when life forces it. What's a limit you'd like to push past?"},
{"title":"Six shortcuts that move people","source_title":"Influence","source_author":"Robert B. Cialdini","topics":["marketing","sales"],
 "body":"People follow mental shortcuts: we return favours, stay consistent with what we've said, copy others, trust experts, say yes to people we like, and want what's scarce. Use them honestly and they build trust; abuse them and you lose it. Which one works on you the most?"},
{"title":"A reason to get up and people to share it with","source_title":"Ikigai","source_author":"Héctor García and Francesc Miralles","topics":["meaning","community","fitness"],
 "body":"Some of the world's longest-lived people, in Okinawa, have a clear purpose, stay active without overdoing it, and belong to tight groups of friends who look out for each other for life. Purpose and people go together. Who's in your crew?"},
{"title":"Shared stories let strangers work together","source_title":"Sapiens","source_author":"Yuval Noah Harari","topics":["big-questions","philosophy"],
 "body":"Humans can cooperate in huge numbers because we believe the same stories: money, companies, nations, laws. They exist because we agree they do, and that agreement is a kind of superpower. What story do you and your people believe in?"},
{"title":"Passion plus staying power beats talent","source_title":"Grit","source_author":"Angela Duckworth","topics":["leadership","careers","coaching-youth"],
 "body":"Talent counts, but effort counts twice: it builds skill, then turns skill into results. Grit is sticking with something you care about for years, practicing deliberately and getting back up. What have you stuck with the longest?"},
{"title":"A little nature goes a long way","source_title":"The Nature Fix","source_author":"Florence Williams","topics":["hiking","camping","fishing"],
 "body":"Research on time outside shows even short doses of nature lower stress and improve mood and focus, and more time does more. Trees, water and quiet are good for your head, not just your weekends. Where do you go to reset?"},
{"title":"Start smaller than you think","source_title":"Rework","source_author":"Jason Fried and David Heinemeier Hansson","topics":["small-business","startups"],
 "body":"Long-term plans are mostly guesses. Start small, launch something real, cut what isn't essential, and stay lean so you can change course cheaply. You don't need investors or a big team to build something good. What could you launch in the next 30 days?"},
{"title":"Your next big break probably comes from an acquaintance","source_title":"The Strength of Weak Ties","source_author":"Mark Granovetter","topics":["networking","careers","community"],
 "body":"A famous study found people more often found jobs through acquaintances than close friends. Your close circle knows what you know; looser ties connect you to new people and new chances. Who's someone you've met once that you should talk to again?"},
{"title":"You can only keep about 150 people close","source_title":"Friends","source_author":"Robin Dunbar","topics":["community","big-questions"],
 "body":"Our brains can keep up meaningful relationships with roughly 150 people, in layers: about 5 closest, 15 good friends, 50 friends, 150 meaningful contacts. Friendships fade without time together. Who in your circles deserves more of your time?"},
{"title":"Life isn't short, we waste it","source_title":"On the Shortness of Life","source_author":"Seneca","topics":["stoicism","meaning"],
 "body":"Two thousand years ago Seneca argued we have plenty of time; we just let others take it and put off living until 'later'. Guard your time like money, and spend it on what you actually value. What would you do this year if you stopped putting it off?"},
{"title":"Ideas want company","source_title":"Where Good Ideas Come From","source_author":"Steven Johnson","topics":["entrepreneurship","community","reading"],
 "body":"Breakthroughs rarely come from a lone genius in a flash. They grow slowly, from half-ideas that collide with other people's half-ideas in shared spaces like coffee houses and labs. Put yourself where ideas mix. Whose half-idea would complete yours?"}
]$json$::jsonb, 'ai');
